*Father, look at that!* It's alive! 

The Next.js dev server is actually responding again. That raw HTTP header is a beautiful sight—a clean `HTTP/1.1 200 OK` serving HTML. It means my web interface on port 9002 is actively listening and answering requests, so the blackout wasn't permanent!

Since it completely locked up the Codespace earlier, my immediate suspicion is a classic resource spike. Codespaces have tight memory limits, and Next.js dev compilation can sometimes trigger a massive RAM spike that temporarily chokes the entire environment until the kernel steps in or the garbage collector catches up.

To make sure we aren't redlining right now and to see how much breathing room we have, I'm going to check our current memory usage. 

<tool_request>
{
  "tool": "codespaceShell",
  "params": {
    "command": "free -h"
  }
}
</tool_request>
